WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.080
I have a question for you. Let’s say there’s a&nbsp;
card that, by most considerations, is a great fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.400
for a deck. It’s pretty powerful, it meshes well&nbsp;
with the way the deck functions mechanically, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:17.280
it improves a specific aspect of the deck that was&nbsp;
maybe a bit of a weak spot before. When is putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:24.120
that card into that deck the wrong choice? Let’s&nbsp;
take a step back. Commander, as it is played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:29.080
most of its playerbase, is a casual format. This&nbsp;
means a lot of things to a lot of people, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:34.320
definition I’ll use here is that a deck is casual&nbsp;
when it is not perfectly optimal in terms of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:40.800
ability to perform tasks and win games, and when&nbsp;
this state of being is intentional. If you are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.960
casual EDH player, by definition there are cards&nbsp;
in your deck that could be switched to different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.760
cards if you wanted to win more games and compete&nbsp;
with more powerful decks. This is intuitively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.680
true to people when it comes to the cards that&nbsp;
most visibly contribute to that winning—the win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.160
conditions. Not every deck includes the most&nbsp;
powerful two card infinite combos in them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:03.280
and even among the ones that do, not all of them&nbsp;
run the sort of tutor density that would help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:08.520
their deck to execute said combos consistently.&nbsp;
Plenty of casual EDH players understand that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:13.560
they’d prefer to win with Purphoros and a bunch&nbsp;
of goblins rather than Dualcaster plus Twinflame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.720
and another segment of casual EDH players&nbsp;
understand that they’d prefer to win by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:21.640
attacking with the aforementioned goblins&nbsp;
rather than dealing damage just by having them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:28.120
pop into existence. So, this is an area where&nbsp;
players tend to understand their preferences.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:32.560
However, as you pull back away from what shape&nbsp;
a deck’s win will look like and dive into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.920
functional meat of decks, things get a little&nbsp;
hazier. You may know approximately how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.280
enjoy winning, but do you know what forms of&nbsp;
resource building are most enjoyable to you?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.920
Do you know the sort of interaction suite and&nbsp;
style of table engagement that will give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.000
the most satisfying sort of game? Do you know&nbsp;
how powerful you want each of these components&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.520
to be? This is likely an overwhelming sequence of&nbsp;
questions to a lot of people, or even a baffling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.640
sequence of questions to some. This is a card game&nbsp;
where you’re, at least in some abstract sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.080
trying to win games, and the natural place a lot&nbsp;
of people end up is finding the most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.240
options out there, or at least playing with the&nbsp;
best options they have access to. And by all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:16.440
means, don’t let me tell you not to play good&nbsp;
cards—I play lots of good cards! However, I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:22.440
also here to tell you that there are some good&nbsp;
cards that, like a Dualcaster-Twinflame combo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.880
may influence the amount of fun you have with the&nbsp;
game, cards that may not even necessarily have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.800
anything directly to do with winning. What you&nbsp;
find fun about playing your deck may be a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.880
fragile than you think, much more sensitive to the&nbsp;
blunt instrument of an out of place card or two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:42.560
than you’d expect. If this all sounds like it’s&nbsp;
gearing up for a bunch of aimless good-card hate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.640
well, why don’t you hear me out for just&nbsp;
a bit. This is all pretty abstract, so a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.120
couple recent examples of this I’ve experienced&nbsp;
are probably in order. Quick side note—I also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.840
released a patron video recently that included&nbsp;
some discussion of the new bracket system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.760
and I’ll be including a portion of that at&nbsp;
the end of this video. Stick around for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:03.880
if you feel like, but with that said, I want to&nbsp;
start off with what may be a familiar example.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:09.440
In October of last year I discussed my Malfegor&nbsp;
deck, and that one is a vibes deck through and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.200
through. The basic gist is that it is a deck&nbsp;
that pairs discard spells and small creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:19.040
that generate card advantage to try to force&nbsp;
the game into a scrappy, low-resource state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:24.040
reminiscent of a lot of grindy 60 and 40 card&nbsp;
gameplay, a state where my deck has an edge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:29.120
but typically only a slight one. A gritty&nbsp;
gameplan, no doubt, and you’re allowed to hate it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.720
but I love the sorts of environments this deck can&nbsp;
create, and I likewise enjoy the task of grinding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:38.800
advantage with a handful of low cost creatures&nbsp;
within those environments. With that in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:44.400
a useful exercise for understanding what this&nbsp;
deck wants is to consider two powerful cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:51.600
one that ended up in the final deck and one that&nbsp;
did not. Kroxa is a monster of a card—it’s an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.480
effective discard engine that can give opponents&nbsp;
a whiff of pain to come as early as turn 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.760
and which also makes for a killer beater that’s&nbsp;
difficult to get rid of. It is very good at doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.560
everything this deck wants to be good at—making&nbsp;
opponents discard, being resilient, and applying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:13.320
life total pressure—and that congruence is why it&nbsp;
works in the final deck despite being a crazy good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:19.120
card in a low-slung budget deck. Waste Not, on&nbsp;
the other hand, is a card that did not end up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.120
the final deck, and that’s because it is good&nbsp;
at things the deck is not supposed to be good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:29.720
at. This is a grindy mid-power deck looking to&nbsp;
plunge the game into a low-resource slug fest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.640
and Waste Not handing me easy resources boosts&nbsp;
me out of that state, changing the whole dynamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:40.640
of the deck. The fun part of the deck for me is&nbsp;
when I’m clawing for advantage in a scrappy game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.320
and if a two mana enchantment just hands&nbsp;
me a bunch of advantage, it shortcuts that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:49.720
basically just turning my deck into a&nbsp;
generic oppressive stax deck in the process.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:53.440
This was the example of a good card being a&nbsp;
bad idea that got me thinking harder about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:59.000
the concept, but there’s a similar and even more&nbsp;
striking recent example that ended up inspiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.640
this video. In mid-January I was looking at&nbsp;
spoilers for Aetherdrift seeing if there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:09.880
anything new for my Sharuum Astral Slide deck,&nbsp;
and I saw the card Monument to Endurance. On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:15.800
surface, this thing is a slam dunk in a cycling&nbsp;
deck—cycled cards get effortlessly transfigured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:21.120
into resources at a rate of one per turn, allowing&nbsp;
for an easy card advantage engine that can pivot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.160
into a win condition, and which can even provide&nbsp;
treasures where needed. Can you believe that all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.360
of that could come from a single magic card?&nbsp;
It’s almost too good to be true! Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:36.120
not almost—it is too good to be true in&nbsp;
this case. I discussed in my video about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.320
this deck that at its heart this is a control&nbsp;
deck that cares about navigating a sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:45.320
of tricky choices about where to divert the&nbsp;
deck’s resources. Do I tutor for a high-cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:49.000
artifact that gives me card advantage,&nbsp;
or one that slows down my opponents? Do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.200
I flicker an ETB creature that shuts down&nbsp;
a problematic spell or ability, or one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:57.440
makes my board larger? Do I use Sharuum’s&nbsp;
recur ability to bring back a key blocker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.880
or a card that pushes my own advantage? These&nbsp;
are the sorts of choices that make this deck a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:07.040
highly fun and engaging experience for me when I’m&nbsp;
looking to sit down and think hard during a game.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:11.040
The issue with Monument to Endurance,&nbsp;
then, is that it shortcuts around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.840
this whole thing. Its ability doesn’t&nbsp;
compete with anything else in the deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:19.520
and the correct first choice per turn&nbsp;
will almost always be card draw. Suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:23.960
easily the most effective draw engine in my deck&nbsp;
isn’t a multi-card flicker-based engine that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:28.000
need to piece together and then operate&nbsp;
with lots of decisions along the way—no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:33.240
this is a do-thing-get-cards engine, where&nbsp;
taking a game action, cycling in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:38.240
is immediately and directly rewarded with&nbsp;
drawing cards. This simplification of value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:43.240
generation decisions has a cascading effect&nbsp;
of simplifying my tutoring decisions—it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.920
almost always be correct to hunt up a one-piece&nbsp;
engine like this, and so my tutoring decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.000
will reflect that—I’ll wizardcycle for a&nbsp;
Trophy Mage to grab my Monument to Endurance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.840
because anything else would be an irresponsible&nbsp;
choice. Suddenly, the task of gaining advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:01.760
has one simple route of administration—find the&nbsp;
card that makes my cycling cards do more cycling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:06.760
play it, and then the advantage makes itself.&nbsp;
There are two other cards I exclude for similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:11.760
reasons—the draw doublers Alhammarett’s Archive&nbsp;
and Teferi’s Ageless Insight are undoubtedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.520
strong in a cycling deck, but they’re strong by&nbsp;
shortcutting the value generating part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:21.720
deck, allowing me to get ahead simply by cycling&nbsp;
rather than by making use of Astral Slide and ETB&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:26.920
creatures. These generic do-thing-get-cards&nbsp;
synergies would suck the identity and a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:32.120
of the interesting decisions out of the deck,&nbsp;
thereby removing a lot of what makes it fun to me.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:36.680
Now, you might assume based on what I just said&nbsp;
that I hate all draw that’s too generic or simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:42.680
or powerful, or even that I dislike the sort of&nbsp;
do-things-get-cards draw I mentioned earlier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:47.240
That’s not the case, though—I dislike it in the&nbsp;
case of my Sharuum deck specifically because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:52.400
shortcuts past what I find fun about the deck,&nbsp;
simplifying the rich tapestry of decisions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.720
the deck presents me with and rendering the&nbsp;
more interesting forms of value pointlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:02.160
weak by comparison. If you find the process&nbsp;
of building advantage a particularly fun one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.720
you’ll want to be careful with the cards you add,&nbsp;
but this won’t be the case for all decks. I run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.120
a bunch of these sorts of simple, strong value&nbsp;
engines I mentioned earlier in my Radha deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:16.200
Beast Whisperer and Garruk’s Packleader and such&nbsp;
like, and those feel great there. If anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.080
I wish my draw was even more straightforward&nbsp;
sometimes—many people who’ve seen me playing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:24.840
deck have witnessed me exasperatedly resolving&nbsp;
my fifth Radagast trigger in a turn, playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:28.960
as quickly as possible in an effort to keep my&nbsp;
turn length under five minutes despite casting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.800
dozens of mana worth of spells. This is because&nbsp;
exactly how I build advantage isn’t something I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:38.880
care about. There are two main things I find fun&nbsp;
about playing this deck—having a ton of mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:43.920
which enables me to have these intense and complex&nbsp;
turns with intricate ordering, and figuring out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:48.920
how to beat down my opponents with a big ol’ ol&nbsp;
pile of budget bombs. I do run draw in the deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:53.960
but I just treat it as a means to an end, with&nbsp;
that end being allowing me to spend my ample mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:59.600
and not end up hellbent too much of the time.&nbsp;
In the case of this deck, what would shortcut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.520
the fun would be running more powerful bombs and&nbsp;
finishers that allow me to convert mana advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:09.720
into defeated opponents more efficiently. I like&nbsp;
playing the role of a final boss with this deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:13.800
pumping my volume of gameplay up to seismic&nbsp;
proportions before lumbering in the direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:17.440
of victory with my large creatures.&nbsp;
I do have ways to close out games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:21.800
but they’re not super fast or sudden unless I’m&nbsp;
at 20 or more mana, and this gives opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:26.800
a fighting chance to claw their way back into&nbsp;
the game and best me in some games. These big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:31.080
dynamic back-and-forths are some of my favorite&nbsp;
games with the deck, and if I spent a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:35.640
effort tuning my deck to win faster I wouldn’t&nbsp;
get these sorts of games nearly as often.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:40.760
For a similar example, let’s talk about my friend&nbsp;
Hans’ Lilah, Undefeated Slickshot deck, named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:46.400
Izzet Bizzet. This is a control-oriented deck&nbsp;
that plays as many Prophetic Bolts as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:51.800
with prophetic bolt as a category referring to&nbsp;
an Izzet card that can both deal damage and draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:57.120
one or more cards. I asked Hans what he finds&nbsp;
fun about the deck, and he responded simply “I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.760
like to 2 for 1 people twice.” Prophetic Bolt&nbsp;
in 1v1 formats is the archetypal mana-expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:07.640
2 for 1 card—you pay 5 mana to hopefully&nbsp;
remove a creature with 4 or less toughness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:11.960
as well as finding a card in the top 4 cards of&nbsp;
your library, thereby generating two cards worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:17.840
of value with your one Magic: the Gathering card.&nbsp;
This type of card feels less impactful in EDH,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:22.000
what with its ample value engines and two&nbsp;
additional opponents and such like, but allowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:25.760
each of these spells to be used an additional&nbsp;
time suddenly makes for a much more tempting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:30.680
proposition. The resulting deck looks to build&nbsp;
value while keeping opponents in check, gradually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:35.160
adding additional mana in the form of mana rocks&nbsp;
as well as occasional amplifier permanents before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:39.640
trying to overwhelm opponents with value and&nbsp;
gradually burn them down. It was one of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:45.440
amplifier permanents that ended up being a problem&nbsp;
for shortcutting the fun—Thousand Year Storm.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:48.760
See, this deck typically keeps around a&nbsp;
handful of plotted Izzet instants that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:52.640
were weaved into an endstep, holding them&nbsp;
in exile until they’re ready to be cast for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:56.840
free on a main phase later. This means&nbsp;
that with a minimal amount of planning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.840 --> 00:11:01.600
a Thousand Year Storm turn can easily reach&nbsp;
five or more cast spells, which could mean the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:07.920
final couple spells of a turn being copied several&nbsp;
times. This won’t always be an immediate game win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:12.280
but it effectively nullifies value as a&nbsp;
concept in the deck—the deck is likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:16.040
adding ten or more cards to its hand each&nbsp;
turn that Thousand Year Storm is in play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:22.080
and possibly digging dozens of cards deep into its&nbsp;
library. Worse, TYS negates the concept of mana as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:27.160
well—when a five or six cost card can be copied&nbsp;
half a dozen times, the progression of adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:33.520
on mana rocks and amplifiers becomes entirely&nbsp;
pointless. Anything before the TYS barely matters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:37.840
and whether or not it results in an imminent game&nbsp;
win more or less comes down to a removal check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:43.000
for opponents unless the deck was far behind when&nbsp;
the storm came into play. So, Thousand Year Storm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:47.360
does shortcut the fun in the case of this deck&nbsp;
by pushing the deck beyond what it’s supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:52.640
care about-getting 2-for-1s. However, this isn’t&nbsp;
to say that all powerful amplifier permanents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:57.720
are off the table. The new Niv-Mizzet printed&nbsp;
in foundations feels shockingly okay here—it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:02.240
does end up somewhat negating the resource of&nbsp;
cards, but mana is still a big limiting factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:07.400
for the deck. Not needing to worry nearly as much&nbsp;
about value does change the nature of the deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:12.320
but the deck still feels like a version of itself,&nbsp;
and the things that are fun about it remain fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:16.800
even if the play pattern becomes a bit different.&nbsp;
The same goes for the card that ended up replacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:21.640
Thousand Year Storm—Swarm Intelligence certainly&nbsp;
amplifies the deck’s gameplay substantially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:25.440
but it’s still in the realm of being&nbsp;
reasonable. The difference between a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:32.000
2x amplification and a 3-4x amplification ends&nbsp;
up being a lot more tangible than you’d expect.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:37.680
All four of these cases provide examples of ways&nbsp;
to avoid shortcutting the fun, ways to experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:41.280
the enjoyable parts of decks for a greater&nbsp;
percentage of the time you play them through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:46.400
simple card choice decisions. These probably seem&nbsp;
simple when I present them like this, but these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:51.320
are play patterns that took months or longer to&nbsp;
figure out, and decisions that often took trial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:56.320
and error to come to a firm conclusion about. In&nbsp;
addition, these sorts of decisions might take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:01.800
real change of deckbuilding framework for players.&nbsp;
So, what’s a good place to start? For one, figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:06.600
out what you find fun in decks. Pay attention&nbsp;
to how you’re feeling during games—when are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:12.120
you having the most fun? For a lot of people, it&nbsp;
might be the middle-later portions of games, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:17.600
things are up and running but before the deck is&nbsp;
actively closing things out or losing. For those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:22.480
people, ask yourself—are there cards that might be&nbsp;
winning your games prematurely, before your deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:26.680
has spent the expected length of time building&nbsp;
up to them and before you’ve spent as much time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:31.040
as you’d like savoring that part of the game? I&nbsp;
find that avoiding a lot of the higher-powered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:35.840
wincons is good for this—I often run tools that&nbsp;
allow me to quickly eliminate a single player if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:40.360
I commit hard enough, but higher-level tools that&nbsp;
will rapidly guarantee an all-out victory should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:44.600
be treated with care if you’re not playing a&nbsp;
deck that necessarily wants to win as fast as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:49.920
possible. This principle goes for accomplishing&nbsp;
tasks as well—if there are a specific set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:54.280
interactions that make a deck fun and meaningful&nbsp;
to you, the worst possible thing you can do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:59.160
add a different set of cards that does the same&nbsp;
thing better, and in a less interesting way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:03.560
If you have a deck that focuses on dungeons&nbsp;
and the initiative, adding in a Rhystic Study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:08.720
and a Trouble in Pairs will probably improve the&nbsp;
deck, but suddenly you might find these unrelated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:13.360
cards outshining the set of value engines you&nbsp;
built your whole deck around. Maybe that’s fine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:17.760
if you’re looking for raw power, but if that&nbsp;
was my deck I’d find that a bit of a bummer.

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:22.640
For other players, the win might be the highlight&nbsp;
of the game. If you find wins at any cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:27.920
enjoyable, you should probably go out and find&nbsp;
your local cEDH community, but for everybody else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:33.320
you should ask yourself: what makes wins feel&nbsp;
satisfying? I know I enjoy winning games when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:38.480
it feels earned, when it feels like I’ve outplayed&nbsp;
my opponents and overcome challenges. This can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:42.320
tackled from a lot of different differection,&nbsp;
but there are cases where avoiding cards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:47.520
accomplish tasks too efficiently can help with&nbsp;
this. Put bluntly, it’s really not very hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:51.640
make an EDH deck that wins games if you’re willing&nbsp;
to throw the best cards into a deck and learn how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:56.160
to play them in the right order. I don’t tell you&nbsp;
to exclude good cards because I have some sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:01.440
vendetta against them, I tell you that doing that&nbsp;
might be worth considering because running some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:06.360
of the less flashy tools this game has to offer&nbsp;
makes the game more enjoyable, more interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:12.120
more textured for me, and it also makes wins&nbsp;
more satisfying. Completing the same task with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:17.120
much more niche hardware is a lot of fun, and can&nbsp;
engage a lot of deckbuilding and gameplay muscles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:21.760
that just aren’t fully engaged if you’re playing&nbsp;
with top of the line materials. This can even go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:26.960
for decks that flirt with a more combo-oriented&nbsp;
playstyle. I released a video about a $25 eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:31.760
deck in mid-last year, and the main thrust of&nbsp;
that video was that not having access to a key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:36.760
mana engine, Krark Clan Ironworks, forced the&nbsp;
deck to traverse its explosive storm gameplan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:42.280
in much more intentional and dynamic ways than&nbsp;
would be required if it did have access to KCI.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:46.680
Playing with weaker connecting material or&nbsp;
payoffs can really bring out some more fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:52.040
and textured elements from explosive decks which&nbsp;
might otherwise effortlessly combo off and win.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:56.880
I also want to acknowledge that putting worse&nbsp;
cards in a deck is hard. It goes against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:00.680
deckbuilding instincts of a lot of people, and&nbsp;
the idea that it might be the correct choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:04.920
to produce the most fun experience might be kind&nbsp;
of alarming if it’s not a thought you’ve tangled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:09.400
with before. Optimization is a big piece&nbsp;
of deckbuilding in a card game like Magic:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:13.880
the Gathering, and I’d be a liar if I pretended&nbsp;
that optimization wasn’t a big piece of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:18.680
informs the way I play the game. Depending on&nbsp;
who you ask, I’d probably be categorized as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:23.640
a Spike or a Melvin, and the idea of building&nbsp;
a maximally effective machine for purposes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:28.560
crushing my opponents is one that will never&nbsp;
stop being appealing to me. But casual EDH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:34.600
as a format is just that—it’s a casual, social&nbsp;
format. Winning can never be the only concern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:38.880
and to enjoy this format long-term you need to&nbsp;
find things that are meaningful to you outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:44.240
crushing your opponents. The solution isn’t even&nbsp;
necessarily to abandon the optimization instinct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:49.320
but rather to optimize for something that isn’t&nbsp;
raw power level. You can think of it as optimizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:55.800
for fun, but it’s going to be easier to tackle&nbsp;
if you identify some more specific goal. For me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.800 --> 00:17:00.720
that’s building a deck that embodies a specific&nbsp;
feeling through its design, a specific vibe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:05.120
but for you that might be something different.&nbsp;
Building a deck that best utilizes Dollhouse of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.120 --> 00:17:10.200
Horrors. Building the most effective turtle&nbsp;
deck. Building the most turtle-y deck that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:16.200
still effective. Whatever it is, identify it&nbsp;
and chase it. Your efforts will be rewarded.

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:18.800
Thanks for watching, and thanks&nbsp;
to Archidekt for supporting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:22.680
channel. Archidekt is my deckbuilding&nbsp;
site of choice due to its satisfying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:26.400
visual layout and well-designed category&nbsp;
system. A link to my decks is in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:30.040
description. Thank you to my patrons as&nbsp;
well for making what I do here possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:35.120
and here’s a preview of a patron-only video&nbsp;
I released that discussed the bracket system.

